course: world
lesson: 40
title: Thinking like a scientist: the body and physics
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Many puzzles about the body are solved by joining what we know about the body with what we know about energy, heat, light and sound.
explain: When you run, your leg muscles burn more food for energy, so they need more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide. That is why you breathe faster and your heart beats faster: to bring oxygen in and carry the waste out faster.
explain: Burning food also makes heat, so you get hot. Sweat cools you because water takes heat from the skin when it evaporates. When you are cold, the opposite happens: muscles shiver, and shivering makes heat.
explain: A fever is not the illness itself but one of the body's weapons: many germs multiply more slowly when it is warmer, and the body's defences work faster.
explain: Light travels about a million times faster than sound, so we see lightning almost at once but hear the thunder later; sound takes about 3 seconds to go one kilometre (about 5 seconds to go one mile), so counting the seconds tells you how far away the storm is.
fact: Working muscles need more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide.
fact: During exercise we breathe faster and the heart beats faster to deliver oxygen and remove waste.
fact: Muscles make heat when they work.
fact: Sweat cools the body because evaporating water takes heat away from the skin.
fact: Shivering is the muscles working quickly to make heat.
fact: A fever is one of the ways the body fights germs.
fact: Light travels much faster than sound.
fact: Sound travels about one kilometre in 3 seconds through air.
fact: Sound travels about one mile in 5 seconds through air.
fact: High on mountains each breath holds less oxygen, so climbers breathe harder.
fact: When you are cold, blood vessels in the skin narrow so less heat is lost from the skin.
fact: The inner ear has fluid that tells the brain how the head is moving; after spinning, the fluid keeps moving and you feel dizzy.
exercise: Why do you breathe faster when you run? | Your muscles need more oxygen and make more carbon dioxide | Running squeezes the air out of your lungs | Your lungs get smaller when you run | Air is thinner when you move fast
exercise: Why does your heart beat faster during a race? | To carry oxygen to the working muscles faster | Because it is frightened of the other runners | To slow the blood down | To keep the bones warm
exercise: Why do you get hot when you exercise? | Working muscles make heat | The air rubs against your skin | Moving fast pulls heat in from the air | Sweat makes the body hotter
exercise: How does sweating cool you down? | Drying sweat takes heat away | Sweat is colder than ice when it comes out | Sweat blocks the Sun's light from the skin | Sweat makes the heart beat more slowly
exercise: Why do we shiver when we are cold? | Shaking muscles make heat | Shaking shakes the cold off the skin | The bones are freezing | The cold makes the nerves stop working
exercise: How can a fever help the body? | Many germs multiply more slowly when it is warmer | A fever cools the germs to death | A fever makes new bones | A fever stops the heart from working too hard all day
exercise: Why do we see lightning before we hear thunder? | Light travels much faster than sound | Thunder happens much later than lightning | Our eyes are closer to the sky than our ears | Sound must first warm up
exercise: About how far does sound travel in 3 seconds in air? | About 1 kilometre | About 1 metre | About 100 kilometres | About round the Earth
exercise: A girl in Texas sees lightning and counts 10 seconds until the thunder. About how far away is it? | About 2 miles | About 10 miles | About 50 miles | About 200 yards
exercise: Why do mountain climbers breathe hard even when walking slowly? | Each breath high up holds less oxygen | Mountains have more carbon dioxide than oxygen | The cold makes the lungs smaller | Their backpacks squeeze their hearts
exercise: Why are your fingers pale and cold on a frosty day? | Their blood vessels narrow to save heat | The blood in them has frozen solid in the cold | The fingers have no blood in them at all in winter | The fingers are busy making extra heat for the hands
exercise: Why do you feel dizzy after spinning round and round? | Fluid in the inner ear keeps moving after you stop | The brain gets far too much oxygen from all the turning | The eyes fall asleep | Blood runs out of your legs
exercise: Why does a wet swimmer feel cold in the wind even on a warm day? | Water evaporating from the skin takes heat away | The wind is made of cold water | Wet skin lets the cold in right through to the bones | The Sun cannot shine on wet skin
exercise: After a long run, why do you keep breathing fast for a while? | The body still has to bring in oxygen and get rid of waste | The lungs forgot how to slow down | The heart has stopped for a rest | The air outside is thinner after a run
exercise: Why does your pulse go up when you climb stairs quickly? | The legs need more oxygen-carrying blood | The stairs press up hard against your heart | Climbing makes the blood heavier to push up | The brain needs less blood when you go upstairs
test: A boy in Toronto sees lightning and counts 9 seconds until the thunder. About how far away is the storm? | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 30 kilometres | About 300 metres
test: A dog has no sweat over most of its body and pants on hot days. Why might panting help it? | Water evaporates from its tongue and mouth and takes heat away | Panting blows the Sun away | Panting brings colder blood from the ground | Panting makes the dog's fur thinner
test: A child has a mild fever with a cold. Why do some doctors say a small fever is not always bad? | It is one of the body's ways of fighting the germs | It kills the germs by freezing them with cold sweat | It means the illness is already gone | It makes the child grow faster
test: Two children run the same race: one at the seaside, one high in the mountains. Who breathes harder? | The one in the mountains, where each breath has less oxygen | The one at the seaside, where air is heavier | Both exactly the same, wherever they run | The one at the seaside, because sea air has no oxygen
test: In a cold room you start to shiver without deciding to. What is your body doing? | Making heat with quick muscle work | Trying to get rid of heat | Sending all the blood to the fingers | Turning off the heart to save energy
test: A fireworks rocket bursts far away. You see the flash, then hear the bang. What does this tell you? | Light reached you sooner than the sound | The bang was made a few seconds after the flash | Your ears are slower than your eyes | Sound only travels at night
test: A cyclist wears a sweaty T-shirt on a windy hill and feels chilly. Why? | Sweat evaporates fast in the wind and takes heat away | The wind blows heat into the shirt | Sweat turns to ice on the shirt | The hill is closer to the cold Moon
test: Which is true? | Breathing and heartbeat speed up together to feed busy muscles | The heart slows down whenever we run | We breathe faster only because we are scared | Muscles need less oxygen when they work hard
test: A girl spins on a roundabout and then stands still, but the world seems to keep turning. Why? | The fluid in her inner ear is still moving | Her eyes are still spinning round inside her head | The ground is really turning | Her blood is spinning in her heart
test: Why would a long-distance runner who forgets to drink be in danger on a hot day? | He sweats out water he needs to cool down | Drinking makes the legs heavier and slower | The body makes more water whenever it runs | Sweat is made from fat, not from water at all
test: saw lightning, counted 6 before the thunder, how far is it | About two kilometres | About six kilometres | About half a kilometre | Right overhead
test: why's u sweat when u run, makes no sense | Evaporating sweat takes heat from the skin and cools you | Sweat is the body getting rid of air | Sweat heats you so you run faster | Sweat is water the muscles do not want
test: my sister says shivering means your body's given up on staying warm. true? | No, shivering is muscles working fast to make heat | Yes, it means the body has stopped | Yes, it is only fear of the cold | No, shivering makes you colder on purpose to save energy
test: a video said you get dizzy after spinning cos your brain gets tired. right? | No, fluid in the inner ear keeps moving and confuses the brain | Yes, spinning tires the brain out | Yes, blood leaves the brain | No, the eyes stop working for a while
exam: Identify the principal reason for which respiration and the heartbeat accelerate during exercise. | To supply additional oxygen and remove waste | To reduce the temperature of the muscles while they work | To diminish the need for food | To expel water from the body
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises shivering? | The rapid working of muscles to produce heat | A sign that the body has become excessively hot | The narrowing of the airways in response to cold | The cooling of the body by the evaporation of sweat
exam: It is given that thunder is heard ten seconds after lightning is seen. Ascertain the approximate distance of the storm in miles. | Two miles | Ten miles | Five miles | Fifty miles
exam: Identify the principal reason for which dizziness is felt after spinning. | The fluid of the inner ear continues to move | The eyes have been damaged by light | The blood has ceased to flow to the head while turning | The muscles of the legs are fatigued
